%T A189286 5911097451480,319469892415808,17584481176101952,982222958294603040,
%U A189286 55530668360895219728,3171318959654377396864,182670436050532943578560,10599737781026193970325760,619014530633087163062727000,36353266320338484003053582400,2145559172529803104937217263040,127190916635938933740168015020160
%N A189286 a(n) = (Sum_{k=0..n} C(6k,3k) * C(3k,k) * C(6(n-k),3(n-k)) * C(3(n-k),n-k)) / ((2n-1) * C(3n,n)).
%C A189286 On Apr 19 2011, _Zhi-Wei Sun_ conjectured that a(n) is an integer for every n=0,1,2,.... He proved that a(p-1)=[(p+1)/6] (mod p) for any prime p, and also made the following conjecture:
%C A189286 (i) a(n)^{1/n} tends to 64 as n tends to the infinity.
%C A189286 (ii) For any positive integer n, we have a(n)=0 (mod 8), and a(n)/8 is odd if and only if n is a power of two.
%C A189286 Adamczewski provides a proof abstract regarding a(n) is an integer for n >= 0 in linked article. - _Michael De Vlieger_, Aug 20 2026
%C A189286 Conjecture (ii) is true. Let s(m) be the binary digit sum of m and T(k) = C(6k,3k)*C(3k,k). By Legendre's formula the 2-adic valuation of T(k) is 2*s(k), and that of (2n-1)*C(3n,n) is 2*s(n) - s(3n). If n is a power of 2, the terms k = 0 and k = n of the sum add up to 2*T(n), of valuation 3, and every other term has valuation >= 4, so a(n)/8 is odd. Otherwise s(n) >= 2: pairing k with n-k, the 2^(s(n)-1) carry-free pairs each have valuation exactly 2*s(n)+1 and all remaining terms have valuation >= 2*s(n)+2, so the sum has valuation >= 2*s(n)+2 and a(n) has valuation >= s(3n)+2 >= 4. Integrality of a(n) was proved by Guo (2013). - _Zedan Soorya_, Nov 13 2026
%H A189286 Michael De Vlieger, <a href="/A189286/b189286.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..556</a>
%H A189286 Tom Adamczewski, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.11941">OEIS Open: How many conjectures can language models turn into theorems?</a>, arXiv:2608.11941 [cs.AI], 2026. See p. 15 (proof, Appendix A).
%H A189286 Victor J. W. Guo, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.4877">Proof of Sun's conjecture on the divisibility of certain binomial sums</a>, arXiv:1301.4877 [math.NT], 2013; Electron. J. Combin. 20(4) (2013), #P20.
%F A189286 Recurrence: (n+2)^2*(3n+2)*(3n+4)*(3n+5)*a(n+2) = 16*(2n+1)*(2n+3)*(3n+2)*(18n^2+54n+41)*a(n+1) - 9216*(n+1)^2*(4n^2-1)*(3n+5)*a(n).
%F A189286 a(n) ~ 2^(6*n-1) * (log(n) + 4*log(2) + 3*log(3) + gamma) / (sqrt(3) * Pi^(3/2) * n^(3/2)), where gamma is the Euler-Mascheroni constant A001620. - _Vaclav Kotesovec_, Nov 13 2026
%e A189286 For n=1 we have a(1) = (C(6,3)*C(3,1)+C(6,3)*C(3,1))/C(3,1) = 120/3 = 40.
%t A189286 S[n_] := Sum[Binomial[6*k, 3*k] * Binomial[3*k, k] * Binomial[3*(n-k), n-k] * Binomial[6*(n-k), 3*(n-k)], {k, 0, n}]/((2n-1)*Binomial[3*n, n]);
%t A189286 Table[S[n], {n, 0, 19}]
%Y A189286 Cf. A005809, A066802.
%K A189286 sign,changed
%O A189286 0,2
